--- Advisory ---
[RUNTIME] src/code_forge/cli.py:558-558 - The new --required-cycles flag changes the verify gate threshold. Omitted flag becomes None and falls back to gate.yaml; a positive int overrides the file and can make a one-cycle repo pass or a multi-cycle repo fail; <1 is rejected only in main. Smoke test: run code-forge verify --required-cycles 1 on a one-clean-cycle fixture and expect exit 0; run without the flag on the same fixture and expect exit 1; run with 0 and expect the CLI-error exit.
[RUNTIME] src/code_forge/verify.py:45-45 - read_required_cycles reads .code-forge/gate.yaml without a lock or timeout and silently falls back to DEFAULT_REQUIRED_CYCLES on any exception, malformed YAML, missing verify section, non-int/bool/<1 value, or missing PyYAML import. A concurrent in-place write can produce partial YAML, so the configured threshold is ignored and the default 3 is used, changing pass/fail. Smoke test: set verify.required_cycles: 1 in gate.yaml and run verify on a one-cycle repo; then corrupt gate.yaml and confirm the fallback is deliberate rather than a silent surprise.
[RUNTIME] src/code_forge/verify.py:322-322 - verify now requires required_cycles * 3 receipts and checks only the last required_cycles cycles. It reads .code-forge/receipts with no coordination with the code-forge review writer, so a concurrent writer can leave a partial receipt set and cause a spurious failure; a lowered threshold can also pass before all running cycles are written. Smoke test: with verify.required_cycles: 2, run after only one clean cycle and expect failure with x/6, then after two clean cycles and expect success; also run while a review process is writing receipts.
[RUNTIME] src/code_forge/verify.py:317-317 - run_verify does not validate required_cycles; only the CLI guard does. Direct callers can pass 0 or negative values. With 0, required=0 and the missing-receipts check is skipped, while cycles[-0:] becomes cycles[0:], causing all historical cycles to be checked. With negative values, cycle-list slicing is also wrong. At call time, required_cycles can be None, a positive int from the CLI after its guard, or an arbitrary int from direct callers; hardened can be True or False and both branches use the same last_n logic. Smoke test: call run_verify(..., required_cycles=0) directly and assert it is rejected or fails cleanly.
