[test-assertion] [test-assertion] run_verify accepts required_cycles without validation. Calling it directly with required_cycles=0 computes required=0 and cycles[-0:] selects every cycle, so the gate can pass vacuously; negative values behave similarly. No test covers invalid direct values, and the CLI guard does not protect this public function.
[test-assertion] [test-assertion] No test exercises the new CLI flag --required-cycles. The parsing, the <1 error path returning EXIT_CLI_ERROR, and the value passed through to run_verify are untested; existing tests call run_verify directly, so main() wiring could regress.
[test-assertion] [test-assertion] No success-path test for a configurable required_cycles greater than 1. With required_cycles: 2 only the failure case 3/6 is asserted. A two-clean-cycle set, or cycles [1,2,7] with required_cycles=2, would verify that last_n selects the last N cycles and that messages use N rather than the hardcoded 3.
[test-assertion] [test-assertion] The invalid-value parametrization for read_required_cycles omits list/dict values ([] or {}) and a non-dict verify section (verify: []), so the isinstance(section, dict) and isinstance(n, int) fallback branches are only partially covered.
